			<div class="content" id="post-content-3573047">
									

<p>My eighth SCP, and hopefully not too confusing.</p>
<p>Thanks to Baubius and Waterfire for feedback in chat! I also received offline feedback from peach time.<br />
<span style="color: white">&#8230;</span></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;glossary/definitions</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;glossary/definitions</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>Normative ethics:</strong> The ethical code that binds all rational agents. There is debate over what it is and if it exists.</p>
<p><strong>Rational agent:</strong> In most cases, a person. More broadly, anyone or anything capable of consciously making a moral choice.</p>
<p><strong>The Trolley Problem:</strong> A famous ethical dilemma in which you may (hypothetically) kill one person to save five.</p>
<p><strong>Deontology/Deontological ethics:</strong> The belief that ethics come from duties, or rules. The consequences of a moral choice don't matter; instead, the choice itself does. A strict deontologist would allow the five to die in the Trolley Problem.</p>
<p><strong>Consequentialism/Consequentialist ethics:</strong> The belief that ethics come from, well, the consequences of a moral choice. A strict consequentialist would kill the one in the Trolley Problem. The Foundation is, by and large, consequentialist. The most famous branch of consequentialist ethics is called <strong>Utilitarianism.</strong></p>
</div>
</div>
