			<div class="content" id="post-content-3573239">
									

<p>I have two issues with this article that prompted me to downvote.</p>
<p>The first is that I think the utilitarian/deontological trolley problem is bar none the most cliche ethical/philosophical &quot;dilemma&quot; in modern media today. The Ethics Committee Orientation and black white gray are both, in my view, the extent to which the trolley problem can be played straight in Foundation mythos; these are both, like, six years old, and now everything else has to be a play on it. This was, in my view, too much of just&#8230;the straightforward trolley problem.</p>
<p>Second, I couldn't really understand the logic of the plot. I don't get why there need to be two article formats: there are easier ways to contain the thing. People who are aware of the skip's true nature but do not act to prevent harm being done to it are not ethically responsible (under a deontological ethical framework but not a consequentialist one, ironically enough). So you can just have a team of researchers who know what the thing is, and only have one person responsible for giving the actual order. It's also still totally unclear to me why you would have to send this to an O5, who now has to seclude themselves in the middle of nowhere and commit regular self-harm, as opposed to another member of the ethics committee.</p>

							</div>

								
					
